WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.094 --> 00:00:00.927
- What are we doing today?

00:00:00.927 --> 00:00:01.760
- Season five.

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:02.760
- Oh, sorry.

00:00:04.543 --> 00:00:05.460
- Worth it.

00:00:05.597 --> 00:00:06.469
- Welcome back.

00:00:06.469 --> 00:00:07.402
A lot has changed.

00:00:07.402 --> 00:00:08.455
I moved to New York.

00:00:08.455 --> 00:00:10.500
There's Annie. She's
helping produce the show.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.141
- Anyway, this is the
episode that I put together

00:00:13.141 --> 00:00:14.818
for your return to Los Angeles.

00:00:14.818 --> 00:00:18.780
We're going to be trying three
large-format meats at three

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.034
drastically different price
points to find out which one is

00:00:21.034 --> 00:00:23.002
the most worth it at its given price.

00:00:23.002 --> 00:00:23.835
- Worth it.

00:00:23.835 --> 00:00:25.388
- Often times on menus you'll see your

00:00:25.388 --> 00:00:27.421
appetizer section, your entrees.

00:00:27.421 --> 00:00:31.369
Sometimes there's an extra
special bonus zone where there

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:35.246
is a very expensive, intended
to be shared dish and that's

00:00:35.246 --> 00:00:37.428
kind of what inspired this episode.

00:00:37.428 --> 00:00:39.749
- Eating big stuff like
this, you need friends.

00:00:39.749 --> 00:00:42.896
Why not eat that with my
friends in this car today?

00:00:42.896 --> 00:00:44.751
- Yeah, that was my idea.

00:00:44.751 --> 00:00:45.695
That's not a novel thing.

00:00:45.695 --> 00:00:47.089
- No, I came up with that just now.

00:00:47.089 --> 00:00:49.445
- That was the whole
point of this excursion.

00:00:49.445 --> 00:00:52.028
(upbeat music)

00:00:53.585 --> 00:00:55.337
- My name is David Kuo
and I'm chef/owner of

00:00:55.337 --> 00:00:56.933
Little Fatty and accomplice bar.

00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:58.765
Today we're going to be
eating Chinese barbecue pork.

00:00:58.765 --> 00:01:00.040
- What kind of place is Little Fatty?

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.898
- It definitely appeals to your
old-school Chinese-American

00:01:02.898 --> 00:01:04.558
palate done with a little twist.

00:01:04.558 --> 00:01:06.231
We have walnut shrimp.

00:01:06.231 --> 00:01:07.239
We have orange chicken.

00:01:07.239 --> 00:01:08.750
We also do super authentic
stuff, like right now we have

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:09.744
dan dan mian.

00:01:09.744 --> 00:01:12.559
We use the Chinese-American
as like a gateway drug.

00:01:12.559 --> 00:01:14.101
It gets you in the door but
then you start ordering this

00:01:14.101 --> 00:01:16.646
other stuff and then you
trust us and order more stuff.

00:01:16.646 --> 00:01:18.537
- We're attached to a
bar. What's with that?

00:01:18.537 --> 00:01:21.190
It's kind of unique to get
craft cocktails or mixology

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:22.058
with Chinese food.

00:01:22.058 --> 00:01:23.135
I think that's what sets us apart.

00:01:23.135 --> 00:01:24.664
- What's your favorite drink on that menu?

00:01:24.664 --> 00:01:26.532
- Right now, I think it's the Pearl Diver.

00:01:26.532 --> 00:01:28.202
It's like this macadamia nut, rum and

00:01:28.202 --> 00:01:29.689
brown butter and sugar mix.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:30.522
You can put it on pancakes.

00:01:30.522 --> 00:01:31.355
- [Andrew] Ooohh. Wow.

00:01:31.355 --> 00:01:32.188
- [David] Yeah, it's that good.

00:01:32.188 --> 00:01:34.949
- So this barbecue pork,
what piece of pork is this?

00:01:34.949 --> 00:01:36.530
- The knee. It's called a pork hock.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.007
I love taking an underutilized cut of meat

00:01:39.007 --> 00:01:40.264
and turn it into something magical.

00:01:40.264 --> 00:01:41.616
I think that's the beauty of cooking.

00:01:41.616 --> 00:01:44.728
We use a combination of Shaoxing,
soy sauce, ginger, garlic-

00:01:44.728 --> 00:01:45.834
- [Andrew] And how long
does that braise for?

00:01:45.834 --> 00:01:46.960
- About four to five hours.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.952
We let it sit overnight
and then we air dry it like

00:01:48.952 --> 00:01:50.380
Peking duck so it gets really crispy.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.483
Put it in the fryer for
about 15 minutes and then we

00:01:52.483 --> 00:01:55.333
shellac it with barbecue sauce
- ketchup, honey, hoisin,

00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:57.806
five spice, garlic, ginger
- all the classic flavors.

00:01:57.806 --> 00:01:59.948
So this is like a family
style Chinese barbecue pork

00:01:59.948 --> 00:02:02.188
with buns and coleslaw and hot mustard.

00:02:02.188 --> 00:02:03.485
- [Andrew] About how many people
does this typically serve?

00:02:03.485 --> 00:02:04.748
- [David] Anywhere from four to seven.

00:02:04.748 --> 00:02:06.873
- [Andrew] Why does that
bun work with this pork?

00:02:06.873 --> 00:02:07.706
- [David] I think it's the
perfect vehicle because it allows

00:02:07.706 --> 00:02:10.524
using your hands without
getting your hands dirty.

00:02:10.524 --> 00:02:12.445
You're getting everything in
there, the sauce, the coleslaw,

00:02:12.445 --> 00:02:14.279
the crispy pork and the unctuous soft pork

00:02:14.279 --> 00:02:15.112
all in a little bun

00:02:15.112 --> 00:02:16.766
- Do you have a specialist that you're

00:02:16.766 --> 00:02:17.674
you're buying those buns from?

00:02:17.674 --> 00:02:20.475
- Yeah, it's actually my
cousin in Monterey Park.

00:02:20.475 --> 00:02:21.308
- Oh cool.

00:02:21.308 --> 00:02:22.299
- He owns a bakery.

00:02:22.299 --> 00:02:23.157
- I'm so hungry.

00:02:23.157 --> 00:02:23.990
(David laughs)

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:25.932
No more questions, please. Let's eat.

00:02:25.932 --> 00:02:26.765
(music)

00:02:26.765 --> 00:02:27.735
(ding)

00:02:27.735 --> 00:02:30.402
- [Andrew] Okay, cheers, Steven.

00:02:30.591 --> 00:02:31.424
- Mmmm.

00:02:31.424 --> 00:02:33.369
- Mmmm. We're drinking the Pearl Diver.

00:02:33.369 --> 00:02:36.318
- Oooh. I have nothing in
my stomach right now and I

00:02:36.318 --> 00:02:38.342
felt a jolt of that. Okay.

00:02:38.342 --> 00:02:40.525
- Perfect time to give you
a knife so that you could-

00:02:40.525 --> 00:02:41.358
- Yeah.

00:02:41.631 --> 00:02:43.041
- Come on, crack in there.

00:02:43.041 --> 00:02:43.874
- Whoa.

00:02:44.146 --> 00:02:45.472
- Whoa. Here, I'll help you out.

00:02:45.472 --> 00:02:46.392
Hold the meat down.

00:02:46.392 --> 00:02:47.648
- That's what I'm talking about.

00:02:47.648 --> 00:02:49.118
- Is it wrong that I just
want to eat this like a

00:02:49.118 --> 00:02:50.451
corn on the cob?

00:02:51.502 --> 00:02:52.335
- Whoa.

00:02:54.059 --> 00:02:54.892
- It's finger food.

00:02:54.892 --> 00:02:57.547
I have a feeling you're
going to love this.

00:02:57.547 --> 00:02:59.816
- Mmmm. I'm going to cut you off a piece

00:02:59.816 --> 00:03:00.957
of this meat right here.

00:03:00.957 --> 00:03:02.712
- Okay. Here, I'll help myself to a bun.

00:03:02.712 --> 00:03:03.545
- Here you go.

00:03:03.545 --> 00:03:05.253
- Come on, Steven. I need
some meat on the bun.

00:03:05.253 --> 00:03:06.361
Just a little touch of that.

00:03:06.361 --> 00:03:07.938
Now look at my bun and look at your bun.

00:03:07.938 --> 00:03:09.824
- Because you're a gluttonous pig.

00:03:09.824 --> 00:03:10.918
- Yeah, I'm a big fatty.

00:03:10.918 --> 00:03:13.840
- (laughs) You're not a big fatty.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.423
The mustard? Oh no.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:17.826
Got a big glob cause I'm used to

00:03:17.826 --> 00:03:20.200
just dousing my hot dogs in mustard.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.033
- Cheers.

00:03:21.216 --> 00:03:22.968
(upbeat music)

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:24.383
- Mmmm. This is perfect.

00:03:24.383 --> 00:03:26.532
First of all, anything
wrapped in a bun, I'll eat.

00:03:26.532 --> 00:03:29.631
- This little package has
everything, fatty meat, it's also

00:03:29.631 --> 00:03:31.052
crispy, powerful mustard.

00:03:31.052 --> 00:03:31.885
- Woo.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:32.830
- Do you feel it in your nose?

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:33.663
- Oh, yeah.

00:03:33.663 --> 00:03:35.985
- That's the mustard scale, one to nose.

00:03:35.985 --> 00:03:38.241
- I think what's so great
about this is the crispy skin.

00:03:38.241 --> 00:03:39.074
- Totally.

00:03:39.074 --> 00:03:41.665
- Cause everything is so soft
and then you get to the skin

00:03:41.665 --> 00:03:42.730
and it's like (crunch).

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:45.319
- A lot of the times on a little
sandwich, you're adding the

00:03:45.319 --> 00:03:48.626
texture from something else,
but here the meat itself has

00:03:48.626 --> 00:03:50.481
all the textures represented.

00:03:50.481 --> 00:03:52.774
This is the kind of thing
that I always see on a menu

00:03:52.774 --> 00:03:53.980
and I'm like, oooh, I want to try that,

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:56.002
but I can't order it by myself.

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:57.379
- You know what that reminds me of?

00:03:57.379 --> 00:03:59.598
You want to play basketball, you have to

00:03:59.598 --> 00:04:01.001
get nine other friends.

00:04:01.001 --> 00:04:03.357
- You have to kind of like
scrounge up some people

00:04:03.357 --> 00:04:05.393
to kind of fill out the table.

00:04:05.393 --> 00:04:08.726
Get some bench players coming to dinner.

00:04:08.916 --> 00:04:10.083
- Here you go.

00:04:11.149 --> 00:04:11.982
- Ooohhh.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:14.181
- So Little Fatty? What'd you think?

00:04:14.181 --> 00:04:15.227
It's fun to share a big
piece of meat, right?

00:04:15.227 --> 00:04:16.753
- Hot mustard. Yes.

00:04:16.753 --> 00:04:18.581
- Sometimes you want to feel
like a little fatty, right?

00:04:18.581 --> 00:04:21.090
- And I definitely feel
like a big fatty right now.

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:22.305
- Do you want to feel like something else?

00:04:22.305 --> 00:04:23.138
- Huh?

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:25.541
- I don't know what to call
this fact, you guys, since I

00:04:25.541 --> 00:04:28.455
chose where we're going, I
decided to do a little research

00:04:28.455 --> 00:04:30.223
of myself and see how you like my facts.

00:04:30.223 --> 00:04:31.056
- Okay.

00:04:31.056 --> 00:04:32.056
- Fact time.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:38.673
- What?

00:04:54.568 --> 00:04:58.078
- So what you're saying is
big brain, little teeth?

00:04:58.078 --> 00:05:01.447
- I'm saying we became smarter
when we learned how to cook.

00:05:01.447 --> 00:05:03.355
- I don't know how I feel about this.

00:05:03.355 --> 00:05:04.724
Facts are getting weirder.

00:05:04.724 --> 00:05:07.021
The restaurants are
getting more arbitrary.

00:05:07.021 --> 00:05:09.709
- Okay, look, did you eat
good food already today?

00:05:09.709 --> 00:05:10.542
- Yes.

00:05:10.542 --> 00:05:11.375
- That's all you need to know.

00:05:11.375 --> 00:05:13.306
It's going to happen two more times.

00:05:13.306 --> 00:05:14.139
Worth it.

00:05:17.333 --> 00:05:19.723
(upbeat music)

00:05:19.723 --> 00:05:22.457
- I'm Jonah Freedman, owner
of Freedman's in Silver Lake.

00:05:22.457 --> 00:05:25.297
Daytime is new school Jewish
deli and then at night

00:05:25.297 --> 00:05:26.871
we have a full cocktail bar.

00:05:26.871 --> 00:05:28.635
- The decor is really awesome.

00:05:28.635 --> 00:05:29.468
- Thank you.

00:05:29.468 --> 00:05:30.301
- I'm blown away by the wallpaper.

00:05:30.301 --> 00:05:32.467
- Cool. Thanks. Today, our
chef, Steph, is going to be

00:05:32.467 --> 00:05:34.217
showing you how we make our brisket.

00:05:34.217 --> 00:05:36.381
Brisket is very much tied to Jewish food

00:05:36.381 --> 00:05:38.030
and family celebrations.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:39.870
You always eat it on high holidays.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.798
It's usually kind of a homey dish.

00:05:41.798 --> 00:05:44.924
Our brisket is not the
sort of overly sweet

00:05:44.924 --> 00:05:46.730
traditional Jewish brisket.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:49.261
The presentation is like Jewish
meets Texas barbecue, but

00:05:49.261 --> 00:05:51.994
the preparation I would say
is quite French, actually.

00:05:51.994 --> 00:05:54.378
We braise them for three
to four hours using

00:05:54.378 --> 00:05:56.588
burnt onion, garlic, raw ginger.

00:05:56.588 --> 00:05:57.699
That all goes into the oven.

00:05:57.699 --> 00:06:00.158
Those are taken out,
cryovaced and then held.

00:06:00.158 --> 00:06:03.070
We heat up in the cryovac bag
so it's this very, very tender

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:05.026
meat that kind of falls
apart and the sauce is

00:06:05.026 --> 00:06:06.365
then ladled on top.

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:08.514
It's served with our pom
Freedman, which are like tater

00:06:08.514 --> 00:06:11.748
tots, b and b pickles, rye
bread and smoked bone marrow.

00:06:11.748 --> 00:06:14.000
We wheel the brisket out on
a cart and cut it tableside

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.290
using what we call our dad knife.

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:17.757
- Electric saw with two
blades that go like that?

00:06:17.757 --> 00:06:18.950
- Electric saw, yeah,
two blades like that.

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:20.718
- Yeah, my dad has one of those.

00:06:20.718 --> 00:06:21.711
- Why that knife?

00:06:21.711 --> 00:06:23.342
- When you think about how you
would cut a brisket at your

00:06:23.342 --> 00:06:25.557
house, you would be using a turkey carver.

00:06:25.557 --> 00:06:28.407
To pair that with a very formal
presentation of bringing it

00:06:28.407 --> 00:06:31.155
to a table has this really
nice kind of dichotomy.

00:06:31.155 --> 00:06:32.810
And that's what Freedman's is about.

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:35.485
We have a great martini program,
but you can always get a

00:06:35.485 --> 00:06:37.898
five dollar hot dog at anytime of the day.

00:06:37.898 --> 00:06:38.731
(music)

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:39.954
(ding)

00:06:39.954 --> 00:06:40.957
- This is really beautiful.

00:06:40.957 --> 00:06:42.130
- Welcome to dinner, Steven.

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:42.963
- What is this?

00:06:42.963 --> 00:06:44.380
- It's a martini.

00:06:44.582 --> 00:06:45.552
- Pickle juice.

00:06:45.552 --> 00:06:46.802
- Pickle juice.

00:06:47.118 --> 00:06:47.951
- Oooh.

00:06:47.951 --> 00:06:50.242
- Oh, yeah, best pickly
martini I've ever had.

00:06:50.242 --> 00:06:51.409
- It's a kick.

00:06:51.457 --> 00:06:53.011
- These are the pom de Freedman.

00:06:53.011 --> 00:06:54.761
- I'm using my hands.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:56.204
- [Andrew] Okay.

00:06:56.204 --> 00:06:57.691
- [Steven] Horseradish mayo.

00:06:57.691 --> 00:06:59.139
- Ooh, is that like whipped?

00:06:59.139 --> 00:07:01.536
The texture of that was
also very surprising.

00:07:01.536 --> 00:07:04.817
- Cheers. I stole some
of your sauce there.

00:07:04.817 --> 00:07:08.734
- Adam, try this. It's
like a potato 3D Dorito.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.241
Anyhoo, let's make little sandwiches here.

00:07:13.241 --> 00:07:15.324
- Bone marrow. Oh my God.

00:07:17.033 --> 00:07:18.826
- You wanted bone meat.
Here it is, Steven.

00:07:18.826 --> 00:07:19.772
- Oh my God.

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:21.640
- This is the literal meat of the bone.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:22.788
- Shut up. (slurps)

00:07:22.788 --> 00:07:23.621
- (disgust)

00:07:23.621 --> 00:07:24.454
(laughter)

00:07:24.454 --> 00:07:26.324
- I'm going to do a
couple pickles on here.

00:07:26.324 --> 00:07:27.949
- Cheers, Steven. Big meat adventure.

00:07:27.949 --> 00:07:28.782
- Oh my God.

00:07:28.782 --> 00:07:31.365
(upbeat music)

00:07:33.135 --> 00:07:33.979
- I'm so happy.

00:07:33.979 --> 00:07:36.355
A lot of these flavors
really make sense to me.

00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:39.768
I grew up in an eastern
European immigrant household,

00:07:39.768 --> 00:07:42.308
like pickles, rye bread, a roast.

00:07:42.308 --> 00:07:45.282
Everything is just like the
perfect iteration of it.

00:07:45.282 --> 00:07:48.037
- Nothing is familiar, yet
everything is familiar.

00:07:48.037 --> 00:07:50.556
- Many times I've thought,
while out to dinner, I wish I

00:07:50.556 --> 00:07:52.275
could just make a tiny
sandwich of this food.

00:07:52.275 --> 00:07:53.108
- Mm-hmmm.

00:07:53.208 --> 00:07:54.171
- And here I have that option, you know.

00:07:54.171 --> 00:07:55.670
- I'm still thinking about that saw.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:58.028
- I love it. The electric knife
has this little light on it.

00:07:58.028 --> 00:08:02.108
It's both a spotlight and
this drum roll for your meat.

00:08:02.108 --> 00:08:05.188
It's like (drum roll), here's your meat.

00:08:05.188 --> 00:08:07.438
- (laughs)

00:08:09.794 --> 00:08:10.627
- Cheers.

00:08:10.627 --> 00:08:11.627
- Oh my God.

00:08:14.632 --> 00:08:15.858
- I got us shareable fruit.

00:08:15.858 --> 00:08:18.154
This is just a thing you can
find all over Los Angeles.

00:08:18.154 --> 00:08:20.782
Lime, salt, chili, it's delicious.

00:08:20.782 --> 00:08:22.099
- I want this pineapple right here.

00:08:22.099 --> 00:08:23.932
- Cheers. Ahhh. Ahhhh.

00:08:26.111 --> 00:08:27.806
That was like lightning up my mouth.

00:08:27.806 --> 00:08:29.686
- So Freedman's? Delicious meat, huh?

00:08:29.686 --> 00:08:31.600
- I want to cut everything with that saw.

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:32.703
- You want a saw like that?

00:08:32.703 --> 00:08:33.536
- Yes.

00:08:33.625 --> 00:08:34.462
- I'll get you it for Christmas.

00:08:34.462 --> 00:08:35.296
Will you hold this for me?

00:08:35.296 --> 00:08:36.248
- Yeah, why?

00:08:36.248 --> 00:08:37.352
- Cooking fact.

00:08:37.352 --> 00:08:38.185
- Wait, what?

00:08:38.185 --> 00:08:39.888
- Hold my fruit. Cooking fact.

00:08:39.888 --> 00:08:42.471
- Hold my fruit. Cooking dance.

00:08:45.628 --> 00:08:47.470
- So this is a continuation
of early humans

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:48.981
cooking for the first time.

00:08:48.981 --> 00:08:50.611
- I like it. A two-parter.

00:08:50.611 --> 00:08:51.707
Are we going to get a third fact later?

00:08:51.707 --> 00:08:52.540
- Nope.

00:08:57.582 --> 00:08:58.415
- Whoa.

00:09:00.358 --> 00:09:01.191
No way.

00:09:05.287 --> 00:09:06.120
Whoa.

00:09:06.836 --> 00:09:10.495
- So you coming back to share
these big, shareable meats

00:09:10.495 --> 00:09:14.162
with me has some
prehistoric origin stories.

00:09:14.925 --> 00:09:17.206
- Wow. You are quite the archeologist.

00:09:17.206 --> 00:09:18.206
- Thank you.

00:09:18.357 --> 00:09:20.838
I hope to be kind of like a
food Indiana Jones one day.

00:09:20.838 --> 00:09:22.184
- A Foodiana Jones?

00:09:22.184 --> 00:09:25.546
- (laughs) Did you hear that pun?

00:09:25.546 --> 00:09:26.379
- Okay.

00:09:26.379 --> 00:09:27.212
- Can you say that again?

00:09:27.212 --> 00:09:28.045
- No.

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:33.050
(light music)

00:09:33.296 --> 00:09:36.120
- I'm Adam Perry Lang here at
APL Restaurant in Hollywood.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:37.646
- So, restaurant's pretty new.

00:09:37.646 --> 00:09:40.084
I noticed the established
date on the menu.

00:09:40.084 --> 00:09:42.039
- 1969. That's when I was born.

00:09:42.039 --> 00:09:44.921
This is really a culmnation
of my journey as a chef.

00:09:44.921 --> 00:09:47.861
Beef is my thing. I love the
procurement of it, aging it.

00:09:47.861 --> 00:09:48.694
- [Andrew] You don't like to call it

00:09:48.694 --> 00:09:50.198
a dry-age room necessarily.

00:09:50.198 --> 00:09:51.231
- [Adam] I don't because
it's not necessarily

00:09:51.231 --> 00:09:52.258
dry all the time.

00:09:52.258 --> 00:09:55.277
You want to have the
correct ratio of humidity

00:09:55.277 --> 00:09:56.584
and vaporation off the meat.

00:09:56.584 --> 00:09:59.061
It's all in the details,
in cooking, in life.

00:09:59.061 --> 00:10:01.399
I went to bladesmithing school
just to make sure that last

00:10:01.399 --> 00:10:03.718
point of contact for
the customer is perfect.

00:10:03.718 --> 00:10:06.149
The taste is just so different
when you have a crummy

00:10:06.149 --> 00:10:08.181
blade and I like to do
things with intention,

00:10:08.181 --> 00:10:09.776
down to making my own knives.

00:10:09.776 --> 00:10:11.655
Today we're going to do one of
my signature dishes, which is

00:10:11.655 --> 00:10:13.828
the APL Short Rib with Pickle Salad.

00:10:13.828 --> 00:10:15.658
One of the key ingredients is actually to

00:10:15.658 --> 00:10:17.753
slather it in ballpark mustard.

00:10:17.753 --> 00:10:20.297
I love tying in nostalgia into cooking.

00:10:20.297 --> 00:10:22.855
I put that mustard on very
lightly just so that the beef

00:10:22.855 --> 00:10:26.154
can brown underneath but the
outside can caramelize and

00:10:26.154 --> 00:10:28.393
make the rub stick or creates a crust.

00:10:28.393 --> 00:10:30.927
The crust carries flavor
and it protects the meat

00:10:30.927 --> 00:10:32.713
from drying out in the initial stage.

00:10:32.713 --> 00:10:34.618
- [Andrew] Do you define
this as a smoked meat?

00:10:34.618 --> 00:10:37.093
Is it barbecue or is it something else?

00:10:37.093 --> 00:10:39.011
- [Adam] I call it wood-fired cooking.

00:10:39.011 --> 00:10:40.550
There's good smoke and there's bad smoke.

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:43.027
If you burn too low, all
these different compounds

00:10:43.027 --> 00:10:44.474
don't have the opportunity to get burnt

00:10:44.474 --> 00:10:46.124
off into the atmosphere.

00:10:46.124 --> 00:10:47.354
That's going on the meat.

00:10:47.354 --> 00:10:49.079
If there's going to be
any smoke, you want it

00:10:49.079 --> 00:10:51.319
to be very subtle and over time.

00:10:51.319 --> 00:10:54.564
I put vinegar on this because
vinegar dries and what's

00:10:54.564 --> 00:10:58.272
left is the sour residue
that creates salivation.

00:10:58.272 --> 00:11:00.431
It gets your mouth
excited and ready to eat.

00:11:00.431 --> 00:11:02.310
Vinegar is like nature's MSG.

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:03.531
- [Steven] What we're
doing for this episode

00:11:03.531 --> 00:11:05.101
is kind of a family-style meat.

00:11:05.101 --> 00:11:07.082
Was there an intention there as a thing to

00:11:07.082 --> 00:11:07.915
bring on for a group?

00:11:07.915 --> 00:11:09.327
- Completely. Fire brings people together.

00:11:09.327 --> 00:11:11.648
It's celebratory, like the family roast.

00:11:11.648 --> 00:11:15.151
I think some of the best
things in life are made simply.

00:11:15.151 --> 00:11:16.026
(music)

00:11:16.026 --> 00:11:17.223
(ding)

00:11:17.223 --> 00:11:18.069
- [Steven] Big money, big meats.

00:11:18.069 --> 00:11:20.636
We both got the Blue Dream
cocktail, but I got the mocktail.

00:11:20.636 --> 00:11:25.136
- But we're both living the
dream, the big meat dream.

00:11:26.208 --> 00:11:28.125
You want to taste mine?

00:11:29.087 --> 00:11:29.920
- Oh, yeah yeah.

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:30.929
- Yeah, yours is way juicier.

00:11:30.929 --> 00:11:32.168
Can I point something out to you?

00:11:32.168 --> 00:11:34.418
The booth smells like meat.

00:11:36.658 --> 00:11:37.627
It's leathery.

00:11:37.627 --> 00:11:40.460
Can I serve you some meat, please?

00:11:40.855 --> 00:11:43.702
Wow, look at how tender this
is. I don't need to cut it.

00:11:43.702 --> 00:11:45.302
I can just pull it apart.

00:11:45.302 --> 00:11:46.135
- Cheers.

00:11:46.135 --> 00:11:47.385
- Okay, cheers.

00:11:50.045 --> 00:11:53.240
It's like if pot roast
and barbecue had a baby.

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:56.435
It has this delightful layer of smokiness.

00:11:56.435 --> 00:12:00.107
- When you swallow it, it
still is there and present.

00:12:00.107 --> 00:12:02.948
Which part of this meat is your favorite?

00:12:02.948 --> 00:12:04.093
- Honestly, it's the crust.

00:12:04.093 --> 00:12:06.190
I've never had a better crust.

00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:09.524
Usually, it's more of a shell
than a flavor component, but

00:12:09.524 --> 00:12:14.212
here it's actually quite tender
and there is a rich flavor

00:12:14.212 --> 00:12:17.295
of black pepper, which I really love.

00:12:17.394 --> 00:12:18.227
- Oh my God.

00:12:18.227 --> 00:12:21.296
- Oh my God. This meat is
just surrendering to me.

00:12:21.296 --> 00:12:24.546
- I'm going to just grab a piece. Ohhh.

00:12:25.946 --> 00:12:29.779
Holy smokes. It has
surrendered to my fingers.

00:12:29.976 --> 00:12:32.941
- Here comes the pickle meat sandwich.

00:12:32.941 --> 00:12:35.205
There's something very
beautiful about a plate

00:12:35.205 --> 00:12:37.865
that comes with just meat and pickles.

00:12:37.865 --> 00:12:40.844
To me, those are like
the two core food groups.

00:12:40.844 --> 00:12:42.561
- Vegetables and protein?

00:12:42.561 --> 00:12:43.394
- Pickle and meat.

00:12:43.394 --> 00:12:44.710
- That's the food group is pickle?

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:46.186
- Pickles and meat. Pickles and meat.

00:12:46.186 --> 00:12:48.324
I'm a little lad who
loves pickles and meat.

00:12:48.324 --> 00:12:49.564
- Can I just take this and grab the bone?

00:12:49.564 --> 00:12:50.397
- Sure.

00:12:50.397 --> 00:12:53.064
- Let's see if this falls apart.

00:12:53.296 --> 00:12:54.129
- Oh, wow.

00:12:54.492 --> 00:12:56.992
- Ohhhh. You got all the meat.

00:12:58.567 --> 00:13:00.394
- Looks like my wish is coming true.

00:13:00.394 --> 00:13:01.377
Here you can have-

00:13:01.377 --> 00:13:03.627
- Oh my God. That is crazy.

00:13:03.635 --> 00:13:06.390
This is not fat, but
it's squishy like fat.

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:09.928
- Is this any less satisfying
to you than a steak, because

00:13:09.928 --> 00:13:12.343
you can come to this restaurant
and get a very nice steak.

00:13:12.343 --> 00:13:15.492
- I would probably take the short rib.

00:13:15.492 --> 00:13:17.711
- I think for me it depends
on the day of the week.

00:13:17.711 --> 00:13:20.659
This feels like Sunday
evening shared meat.

00:13:20.659 --> 00:13:22.704
There's also something
really great about having

00:13:22.704 --> 00:13:24.513
a big table where everybody's eating and

00:13:24.513 --> 00:13:26.298
they're all tasting the same thing.

00:13:26.298 --> 00:13:28.140
It's not like oh, mine's
good, how's yours?

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:29.032
Oh, it's good.

00:13:29.032 --> 00:13:31.760
We can look at each other and
we know what it tastes like

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:33.226
because it's in all of our
mouths, like your brains

00:13:33.226 --> 00:13:35.376
connected through this invisible network.

00:13:35.376 --> 00:13:36.209
- Whoa.

00:13:37.398 --> 00:13:38.721
- The meat matrix.

00:13:38.721 --> 00:13:41.043
- This may be the ultimate
group bonding experience.

00:13:41.043 --> 00:13:41.876
- Yeah.

00:13:41.876 --> 00:13:43.110
- Forget escape rooms.

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:44.193
- Group meat.

00:13:54.784 --> 00:13:55.825
- First episode back.

00:13:55.825 --> 00:13:56.658
- You feel good?

00:13:56.658 --> 00:13:57.491
- Yeah.

00:13:57.491 --> 00:13:59.103
- You feel very full, very satiated?

00:13:59.103 --> 00:14:02.057
- I don't think I'll be
eating meat for a day or two,

00:14:02.057 --> 00:14:04.276
but after that detox, back to meat.

00:14:04.276 --> 00:14:06.932
- Which shareable meat was
the most worth it to you

00:14:06.932 --> 00:14:08.215
at its given price?

00:14:08.215 --> 00:14:10.701
- I would have to say Little Fatty.

00:14:10.701 --> 00:14:11.534
- Wow.

00:14:11.534 --> 00:14:13.957
- Yeah. They're all very
good, but Little Fatty's was

00:14:13.957 --> 00:14:17.128
under 30 dollars plus it's
a truly Chinese-American

00:14:17.128 --> 00:14:18.815
restaurant and I love that.

00:14:18.815 --> 00:14:21.117
- Yeah, I think my worth
it winner is Freedman's,

00:14:21.117 --> 00:14:24.550
the best balance of a meat
I'd love to eat on its own

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:25.857
and a meat that's going to be great as a

00:14:25.857 --> 00:14:27.239
little sandwich afterward.

00:14:27.239 --> 00:14:29.591
I took some of that meat home,
turned it into a spaghetti

00:14:29.591 --> 00:14:30.811
sauce. It was phenomenal.

00:14:30.811 --> 00:14:32.761
Adam, who's your worth it winner?

00:14:32.761 --> 00:14:33.594
- [Adam] APL.

00:14:33.594 --> 00:14:34.427
- What?

00:14:34.427 --> 00:14:35.982
- Wow. Good answer. I'll accept it.

00:14:35.982 --> 00:14:38.236
Hey, Annie, who's your worth it winner?

00:14:38.236 --> 00:14:39.568
- I saw that one coming, actually.

00:14:39.568 --> 00:14:42.147
She was scarfing down the
Freedman's after we left.

00:14:42.147 --> 00:14:45.964
- We're going to your neck of
the woods for next episode.

00:14:45.964 --> 00:14:47.168
Want to give us a little sneak peek?

00:14:47.168 --> 00:14:51.165
- Rhymes with hurry and it's
a very bad basketball player.

00:14:51.165 --> 00:14:52.248
LeBron rules.

00:14:52.373 --> 00:14:54.195
(Andrew laughs)

00:14:54.195 --> 00:14:55.754
- [Andrew] So we're going to
need to learn how to share

00:14:55.754 --> 00:14:57.119
before we could cook, and by
the way we're talking about

00:14:57.119 --> 00:14:59.452
possibly a million years ago.

00:14:59.452 --> 00:15:00.893
- [Steven] Why are your facts all about

00:15:00.893 --> 00:15:01.936
millions of years ago?

00:15:01.936 --> 00:15:03.300
- [Andrew] That's when
the best stuff happened.

